<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yorkshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yorkshire is a historic county of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the largest in the country, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. It formed the core of the Anglian kingdom of Deira and later of the Viking Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, whose settlers left hundreds of Scandinavian place-names. Its traditional divisions were the North, East and West Ridings around the city of York.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yorkshire.jpg" alt="Yorkshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kreuzschnabel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2015_Swaledale_from_Kisdon_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Wilberforce, Member of Parliament for <b>Yorkshire</b>, carried the cause into the House of Commons, which voted down his motion in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1811 and 1816 the Luddites of Nottinghamshire, <b>Yorkshire</b> and Lancashire smashed machinery, and in 1812 Parliament made the breaking of stocking frames punishable by death.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Yorkshire</b> croppers broke the cropping machines made by Enoch Taylor of Marsden with a hammer they called “Enoch”, purportedly shouting, “Enoch made them, and Enoch shall break them.”</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its enemies took it up too: at the Battle of Famars in 1793, the British 14th Regiment of Foot attacked the French to its tune, and the regiment was later awarded it as a battle honour and quick march, a tradition the <b>Yorkshire</b> Regiment keeps.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scarborough or Whittingham Fair form was most common in <b>Yorkshire</b> and Northumbria, where it was sung to several tunes, often in the Dorian mode.</blockquote><a href="/songs/scarborough-fair">Scarborough Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>Fought in a snowstorm on Palm Sunday in <b>Yorkshire</b>, Towton is often called the bloodiest battle on English soil, with traditional estimates of 28,000 dead.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-29">29 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
